##:
io/fs -- composed file-system operations, pure Sushi.

The module ships as bundled .sushi source and is merged as a compilation unit
when imported (`use <io/fs>`). It builds on the <io/files> primitives and the
<io/path> algebra: stat() composes the per-field reads into one FileStat,
walk() recurses over read_dir(), and mkdir_all()/remove_all() are the
recursive forms of mkdir() and remove()/rmdir().

Limitations: stat() costs one system call per field. walk() lists regular
files only and does not follow a directory symlink, so a linked tree stays
unvisited and a loop cannot form.
:##

use <io/contracts>
use <io/files>
use <io/path>

##:
The metadata of one path, as stat() composes it.
:##
public struct FileStat:
    ##: The size in bytes. :##
    i64 size
    ##: The modification time, in unix seconds. :##
    i64 mtime
    ##: The inode status-change time, in unix seconds. :##
    i64 ctime
    ##: The raw st_mode: file-type bits plus permission bits. :##
    i32 mode
    ##: Whether the path itself is a symbolic link. :##
    bool is_symlink

##:
Reads the metadata of a path into one FileStat.

Each field is one <io/files> read; the cost is one system call per field.

- Parameter path: The path to inspect.
- Returns: The composed FileStat.
- Errors: FileError, when the path cannot be read.
:##
public fn stat(string path) FileStat | FileError:
    let i64 size = file_size(path)??
    let i64 mt = mtime(path)??
    let i64 ct = ctime(path)??
    let i32 md = mode(path)??
    let bool link = is_symlink(path)??
    return Result.Ok(FileStat(size, mt, ct, md, link))

##:
Walks a directory tree and collects the regular files.

The result holds full joined paths. A directory symlink is not followed, so
a loop cannot form. The order follows read_dir() and is unspecified.

- Parameter path: The directory to walk.
- Returns: The file paths under it, at every depth.
- Errors: FileError, when a directory cannot be listed.
:##
public fn walk(string path) string[] | FileError:
    let List@(string) acc = List.new()
    match walk_into(path, poke acc):
        Result.Ok(_) -> ~
        Result.Err(e) ->
            acc.free()
            return Result.Err(e)
    let i32 n = acc.len()
    let string[] out = from([""; n])
    foreach(i in 0..n):
        match acc.get(i):
            Maybe.Some(p) ->
                out[i] := p.clone()
            Maybe.None -> ~
    acc.free()
    return Result.Ok(out)

##:
Creates a directory and every missing parent.

An existing directory on the way is kept; losing the creation race to
another process counts as success.

- Parameter path: The directory path to build.
- Parameter dir_mode: Permissions for each created level, in octal.
- Errors: FileError, when a level cannot be created.
:##
public fn mkdir_all(string path, i32 dir_mode) ~ | FileError:
    let bool rooted = path.starts_with("/")
    let string[] parts = path.split("/")
    let string prefix = ""
    foreach(part in parts.iter()):
        if (not part.is_empty()):
            if (prefix.is_empty()):
                if (rooted):
                    prefix := "/".concat(part)
                else:
                    prefix := part.clone()
            else:
                prefix := prefix.concat("/").concat(part)
            if (not exists(prefix)):
                match mkdir(prefix, dir_mode):
                    Result.Ok(_) -> ~
                    Result.Err(e) ->
                        match e:
                            FileError.AlreadyExists -> ~
                            _ -> return Result.Err(e)
    return Result.Ok(~)

##:
Removes a path and, for a directory, everything under it.

A missing path is success: the goal state already holds. A symlink is
removed as the link; its target stays.

- Parameter path: The path to remove.
- Errors: FileError, when an entry cannot be removed.
:##
public fn remove_all(string path) ~ | FileError:
    let bool link = is_symlink(path).realise(false)
    if (not link):
        if (not exists(path)):
            return Result.Ok(~)
        if (is_dir(path)):
            let string[] names = read_dir(path)??
            foreach(name in names.iter()):
                let string full = join(path, name).realise("")
                match remove_all(full):
                    Result.Ok(_) -> ~
                    Result.Err(e) -> return Result.Err(e)
            match rmdir(path):
                Result.Ok(_) -> return Result.Ok(~)
                Result.Err(e) -> return Result.Err(e)
    match remove(path):
        Result.Ok(_) -> return Result.Ok(~)
        Result.Err(e) -> return Result.Err(e)

##:
Collects the regular files under one directory into the accumulator.

- Parameter dir: The directory to scan.
- Parameter acc: The collected file paths.
- Errors: FileError, when the directory cannot be listed.
:##
fn walk_into(string dir, poke List@(string) acc) ~ | FileError:
    let string[] names = read_dir(dir)??
    foreach(name in names.iter()):
        let string full = join(dir, name).realise("")
        if (is_dir(full)):
            if (not is_symlink(full)??):
                match walk_into(full, poke acc):
                    Result.Ok(_) -> ~
                    Result.Err(e) -> return Result.Err(e)
        else:
            acc.push(full.clone())
    return Result.Ok(~)

##:
One open file, and the descriptor it owns.

A File moves to exactly one owner and closes itself when that owner leaves
scope, on the same terms as TcpStream: the rule is enforced by the compiler
rather than written down here, and handing one to a `nom` parameter is what
transfers it.

The `owned` bit is what lets the three console handles be Files too. They
name descriptors the program did not open -- 0, 1 and 2 -- so dropping one
must not close it. A string carries the same bit for the same reason: a
literal frees to a no-op.

The contract methods -- read, write, flush, seek and the conveniences over
them -- take `poke self`, the receiver every Reader and Writer declares so that
a BUFFERED handle can implement them (its cursor moves). A File's own position
lives in the KERNEL, so the mode costs it nothing; close() takes the handle.

Reads and writes are UNBUFFERED: each one is a system call. A handle that buffered would
have to flush at a point nobody wrote, and a destructor cannot report the failure of that
flush -- so buffering is the layer above, in <io/buf>, which a caller opts into.
:##
public struct File:
    ##: The descriptor, or -1 once close() has run. :##
    i32 fd
    ##: Whether dropping this handle closes the descriptor. :##
    bool owned

##: The descriptor the kernel gives standard input. :##
public const i32 STDIN_FD = 0
##: The descriptor the kernel gives standard output. :##
public const i32 STDOUT_FD = 1
##: The descriptor the kernel gives standard error. :##
public const i32 STDERR_FD = 2

##:
Standard input, as a File the program does not own.

It is a unit variable (`var`), so it has an address: the contract methods take
`poke self`, and `stdin.read(...)` reaches it. Closing it is still refused
while compiling -- storage the program keeps is never moved out of (CE2436) --
and a rebind (`stdin := f`) is the one way to put the console somewhere else
for a run.
:##
public var File stdin = File(fd: STDIN_FD, owned: false)

##: Standard output, on the same terms as stdin. :##
public var File stdout = File(fd: STDOUT_FD, owned: false)

##: Standard error, on the same terms as stdin. :##
public var File stderr = File(fd: STDERR_FD, owned: false)

##:
Closes the file when its owner leaves scope, if this handle owns it.
:##
extend File with Drop:
    ##:
    Closes the descriptor, unless the handle does not own it.

    A destructor has nowhere to put a Result, so a close that fails here is
    lost. Call close() to see it.
    :##
    fn drop(poke self) ~:
        if (self.owned):
            close_handle(poke self)

##:
Releases the descriptor exactly once, and writes -1 into the handle.

Private to this module, and the ONE place the guard and the fd_close() call are
spelled: close() consumes its handle and cannot be reached from a destructor,
which owns nothing to hand away, so the two callers meet here instead of each
carrying a copy of the rule.

- Parameter h: The handle to release. Reads -1 afterwards.
- Errors: IoError, when the descriptor could not be closed.
:##
fn close_handle(poke File h) ~ | IoError:
    if (h.fd < 0):
        return Result.Ok(~)
    let i32 fd = h.fd
    h.fd := -1
    match fd_close(fd):
        Result.Ok(_) -> return Result.Ok(~)
        Result.Err(e) -> return Result.Err(e.to_io())

##:
What a FileMode asks the platform for.

The O_* flag VALUES differ between macOS and Linux and Sushi has no
conditional compilation, so a number spelled here would be silently wrong on
one of them -- 0x0400 means truncate on one and append on the other. An
INTENT crosses the boundary instead and the platform layer maps it.

POSIX draws no text/binary distinction, so the B twins are not a second
mapping.

- Returns: The intent: 0 read, 1 write, 2 append.
:##
extend FileMode intent() i32:
    match self:
        FileMode.Read -> return 0
        FileMode.ReadB -> return 0
        FileMode.Write -> return 1
        FileMode.WriteB -> return 1
        FileMode.Append -> return 2
        FileMode.AppendB -> return 2

##:
Which end of the file a seek counts from.

- Returns: The whence: 0 the start, 1 the current position, 2 the end.
:##
extend SeekFrom whence() i32:
    match self:
        SeekFrom.Start -> return 0
        SeekFrom.Current -> return 1
        SeekFrom.End -> return 2

##:
Turns a file-system error into the one channel the io contracts answer.

Every FileError variant with no twin in IoError belongs to an open rather than to a read or
a write, so nothing a contract method can answer is lost here. IOError has already thrown
its errno away, so it collapses to Other rather than pretending to carry a number.

- Returns: The IoError that says the same thing.
:##
extend FileError to_io() IoError:
    match self:
        FileError.NotFound -> return IoError.NotFound
        FileError.PermissionDenied -> return IoError.PermissionDenied
        FileError.AlreadyExists -> return IoError.AlreadyExists
        FileError.IsDirectory -> return IoError.IsDirectory
        FileError.DiskFull -> return IoError.DiskFull
        FileError.TooManyOpen -> return IoError.TooManyOpen
        FileError.InvalidPath -> return IoError.InvalidInput
        FileError.IOError -> return IoError.Other
        FileError.Other -> return IoError.Other

##:
Opens a file and takes ownership of its descriptor.

The handle closes itself when its owner leaves scope, so an explicit close() is only needed
where the failure has to be seen.

The answer is on IoError rather than FileError, so a function that opens a file and then
reads it carries ONE channel from end to end.

- Parameter path: The path to open.
- Parameter mode: What the file is opened for.
- Returns: The open file.
- Errors: IoError, when the path could not be opened.
:##
public fn open(string path, FileMode mode) File | IoError:
    match fd_open(path, mode.intent(), 420):
        Result.Ok(fd) -> return Result.Ok(File(fd: fd, owned: true))
        Result.Err(e) -> return Result.Err(e.to_io())

##:
Reads bytes from the file, as one read and as one write.

A File satisfies the contract for both directions, so a generic that asks for Reader takes
a file exactly as it takes a socket.
:##
extend File with Reader:
    ##:
    Reads what one read gives, as text.

    The bound counts BYTES, so a multi-byte character can be split across two calls. A
    caller that must not cut one reads bytes, accumulates them, and converts once.

    - Parameter max: The most bytes to take in this one read.
    - Returns: The text that arrived, empty at end of file.
    - Errors: IoError, when the read failed.
    :##
    fn read(poke self, i32 max) string | IoError:
        let u8[] chunk = self.read_bytes(max)??
        return chunk.to_string()

    ##:
    Reads what one read gives, as bytes.

    An EMPTY answer means end of file, which is what lets a caller loop until the answer is
    empty. A short answer is not an end: a pipe hands over whatever has arrived.

    - Parameter max: The most bytes to take in this one read.
    - Returns: The bytes that arrived, empty at end of file.
    - Errors: IoError, when the read failed.
    :##
    fn read_bytes(poke self, i32 max) u8[] | IoError:
        match fd_read(self.fd, max):
            Result.Ok(nom chunk) -> return chunk
            Result.Err(e) -> return Result.Err(e.to_io())

##:
Writes to the file, and flushes what a descriptor has no need to flush.
:##
extend File with Writer:
    ##:
    Writes text, and does not stop until every byte has gone.

    The string's own bytes go to the kernel with no to_bytes() copy in front of them.

    - Parameter data: The text to write.
    - Returns: Nothing.
    - Errors: IoError, when the write failed.
    :##
    fn write(poke self, string data) ~ | IoError:
        match fd_write_str(self.fd, data):
            Result.Ok(_) -> return ~
            Result.Err(e) -> return Result.Err(e.to_io())

    ##:
    Writes bytes, and does not stop until every one has gone.

    - Parameter data: The bytes to write. The buffer stays the caller's.
    - Returns: Nothing.
    - Errors: IoError, when the write failed.
    :##
    fn write_bytes(poke self, u8[] data) ~ | IoError:
        match fd_write(self.fd, data):
            Result.Ok(_) -> return ~
            Result.Err(e) -> return Result.Err(e.to_io())

    ##:
    Does nothing, successfully.

    A descriptor is not buffered: a write has already reached the kernel by the time write()
    returns, so there is nothing here to flush. The method is on the contract because a
    contract carries ONE signature: a function written against Writer must keep compiling
    when the handle it is handed is swapped for a BufWriter, where the call drains a real
    buffer. Getting bytes onto the DISK is fsync(), a much stronger promise, and not what
    flush() ever meant.

    - Returns: Nothing.
    - Errors: IoError, never in practice.
    :##
    fn flush(poke self) ~ | IoError:
        return ~

##:
Moves the file position. A socket does not implement this perk, because a stream has no
position to move.
:##
extend File with Seek:
    ##:
    Moves the position, and answers where it landed.

    - Parameter offset: How far to move, which may be negative.
    - Parameter origin: Which end to count from.
    - Returns: The new position, in bytes from the start.
    - Errors: IoError, when the descriptor cannot seek -- a pipe cannot.
    :##
    fn seek(poke self, i64 offset, SeekFrom origin) i64 | IoError:
        match fd_seek(self.fd, offset, origin.whence()):
            Result.Ok(at) -> return at
            Result.Err(e) -> return Result.Err(e.to_io())

##:
Reads the rest of the file, from the current position, as text.

The whole answer is held in memory at once. A large file wants read() in a loop, or the
buffered reader.

- Returns: Everything from the current position to the end.
- Errors: IoError, when a read failed.
:##
extend File read_all(poke self) string | IoError:
    let u8[] all = from([])
    let bool done = false
    while (not done):
        let u8[] chunk = self.read_bytes(65536)??
        if (chunk.len() == 0):
            done := true
        else:
            all.extend(chunk)
    return all.to_string()

##:
Reads one line, without its newline.

A blank line is Some("") and the end of the file is None, so the two are never the same
answer. A line is read a byte at a time on a descriptor that cannot seek -- a pipe cannot
give back an over-read -- and in chunks on one that can.

This is the unbuffered read, one system call per line at best. Reading a whole file line
by line wants BufReader from <io/buf>.

- Returns: The line, or None at end of file.
- Errors: IoError, when the read failed.
:##
extend File readln() Maybe@(string) | IoError:
    match fd_readln(self.fd):
        Result.Ok(nom line) -> return line
        Result.Err(e) -> return Result.Err(e.to_io())

##:
Reads one byte, as text.

- Returns: One character, or an empty string at end of file.
- Errors: IoError, when the read failed.
:##
extend File readch(poke self) string | IoError:
    let u8[] one = self.read_bytes(1)??
    return one.to_string()

##:
Writes text and a newline.

Two writes rather than one: joining them would allocate a copy of the line on every call,
and a file descriptor takes the second write for nothing.

- Parameter data: The text to write.
- Returns: Nothing.
- Errors: IoError, when a write failed.
:##
extend File writeln(poke self, string data) ~ | IoError:
    self.write(data)??
    self.write("\n")??
    return ~

##:
The current file position, in bytes from the start.

- Returns: The position.
- Errors: IoError, when the descriptor cannot seek.
:##
extend File tell(poke self) i64 | IoError:
    return self.seek(0 as i64, SeekFrom.Current)??

##:
Reads bytes at an offset, without moving the file position.

The offset is an ARGUMENT, so nothing is shared between two callers and nothing can
race: this is the answer for concurrent reads of one file, where share() is not. One
pread(2), so the answer may be shorter than asked for, and it is empty past the end of
the file.

- Parameter offset: Where to read from, in bytes from the start.
- Parameter count: The most bytes to take in this one read.
- Returns: The bytes that were there, empty past the end.
- Errors: IoError, when the read failed. A pipe cannot be read at an offset.
:##
extend File read_at(i64 offset, i32 count) u8[] | IoError:
    match fd_pread(self.fd, offset, count):
        Result.Ok(nom chunk) -> return chunk
        Result.Err(e) -> return Result.Err(e.to_io())

##:
Writes bytes at an offset, without moving the file position.

One pwrite(2). The bytes it covers are replaced and the rest of the file is left alone.
It answers the count it took, which may be fewer than offered, and the loop is the
caller's, exactly as it is for a socket's send().

- Parameter offset: Where to write, in bytes from the start.
- Parameter data: The bytes to write. The buffer stays the caller's.
- Returns: How many bytes were written.
- Errors: IoError, when the write failed.
:##
extend File write_at(i64 offset, u8[] data) i32 | IoError:
    match fd_pwrite(self.fd, offset, data):
        Result.Ok(n) -> return n
        Result.Err(e) -> return Result.Err(e.to_io())

##:
Opens a second handle over the same open file description.

This is dup(2). The answer is an independent descriptor that this program OWNS: it
closes on drop even when the receiver is a console constant, and closing either handle
leaves the other open. The open file description is SHARED, and the offset is part of
it, so a read through one handle moves the other. That makes share() the
shared-listener pattern and not the answer for concurrent reads of one file; read_at()
and write_at() are that answer, because their offset is an argument.

A handle has no .clone() (CE2431), because a copy verb would hide the second
descriptor. This is the operation that means one, and its name says so.

- Returns: A second owning handle over the same open file description.
- Errors: IoError, when the descriptor could not be duplicated.
:##
extend File share() File | IoError:
    match fd_dup(self.fd):
        Result.Ok(fd) -> return File(fd: fd, owned: true)
        Result.Err(e) -> return Result.Err(e.to_io())

##:
Whether this handle still holds a descriptor.

- Returns: False once close() has run.
:##
extend File is_open() bool:
    return self.fd >= 0

##:
Whether the descriptor is a terminal.

Answers false for a file, and for a pipe or a redirect -- which is what a program asks
before deciding to print colour.

- Returns: True only for a terminal.
:##
extend File is_terminal() bool:
    return fd_isatty(self.fd)

##:
Closes the file, and CONSUMES the handle.

Only for the caller who has to SEE that the close failed: the destructor closes an owned
handle anyway, and cannot answer a Result. The call takes the handle, so a read after a
close is refused while compiling (CE2435) rather than answering EBADF at run time, and
there is nothing left for the scope exit to close.

A handle held in a struct FIELD cannot be closed this way -- a field read is a borrow,
and consuming one is CE2411. Let the struct's own drop close it, or take the handle out
of the wrapper that holds it with into_inner().

- Returns: Nothing. The handle is spent.
- Errors: IoError, when the descriptor could not be closed.
:##
extend File close(nom self) ~ | IoError:
    close_handle(poke self)??
    return ~
